<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Milan Aćimović</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1898–1945</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> Aćimović was a Serbian police official and politician who served as interior minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> in 1939. After the German conquest of 1941 he headed the Commissioner Government in occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, which proved unable to contain the uprising of that summer. He then served as interior minister under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan-nedic">Milan Nedić</a> and was killed in 1945 while with Chetnik forces.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/milan-acimovic.jpg" alt="Milan Aćimović" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: File:Milan Aćimović sh.jpg: Unknown (possible official portrait) derivative work: Begoon · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Milan_A%C4%87imovi%C4%87_sh_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
